1
00:00:00,880 --> 00:00:05,200
So, to summarize these two
sessions on inferential statistics,

2
00:00:05,240 --> 00:00:09,320
we can take an overview
of what we have covered.

3
00:00:09,900 --> 00:00:13,060
First, when you obtain a dataset,

4
00:00:13,160 --> 00:00:16,740
or create one from your observations,

5
00:00:17,400 --> 00:00:23,020
you will describe the
variables available to you.

6
00:00:23,480 --> 00:00:27,540
One important thing to examine is
the distribution of these variables,

7
00:00:27,540 --> 00:00:31,480
particularly whether your variable
of interest, such as plant

8
00:00:31,480 --> 00:00:36,020
growth or the effect of a drug,
follows a normal distribution.

9
00:00:36,140 --> 00:00:39,420
We can examine this graphically,
but we can also use a test

10
00:00:39,420 --> 00:00:44,200
such as the Shapiro test to test our
variables of interest for normality.

11
00:00:44,520 --> 00:00:48,860
If this variable follows a Gaussian
distribution, described as parametric,

12
00:00:49,480 --> 00:00:55,060
we can perform so-called
parametric tests on it.

13
00:00:55,060 --> 00:01:02,560
If, for example, we want to
compare means with a factor

14
00:01:02,560 --> 00:01:07,260
that has two levels, hence two
groups, we can use the t-test.

15
00:01:07,540 --> 00:01:11,360
If we have a factor with more
than two levels, we have ANOVA.

16
00:01:11,520 --> 00:01:15,480
We can also use ANOVA with
several factors, each with several

17
00:01:15,480 --> 00:01:20,700
levels, and examine interactions
between the different factors.

18
00:01:20,920 --> 00:01:24,380
If, on the other hand, our data do
not follow a parametric distribution,

19
00:01:24,560 --> 00:01:31,120
we will prefer the nonparametric
equivalents of these tests: Wilcoxon

20
00:01:31,120 --> 00:01:35,480
instead of the t-test, and
Kruskal-Wallis instead of one-way ANOVA.

21
00:01:35,520 --> 00:01:39,780
There is no nonparametric equivalent
of ANOVA with several factors.

22
00:01:40,460 --> 00:01:44,600
We also saw other types of tests,
based not on differences in means,

23
00:01:44,640 --> 00:01:51,960
but rather on the dependence
between different categories

24
00:01:51,960 --> 00:01:57,060
and on the population counts
in those different categories.

25
00:01:58,200 --> 00:02:04,640
To do this, we compare the expected
counts with the observed counts

26
00:02:04,640 --> 00:02:10,260
and typically use the chi-square
test for these analyses.

27
00:02:11,400 --> 00:02:15,500
Finally, all the statistics we
have seen, from the simplest to the

28
00:02:15,500 --> 00:02:20,360
most complex, from a mean to a chi-square
statistic, can be bootstrapped.

29
00:02:20,440 --> 00:02:24,760
That is, we take our original
sample and generate dozens,

30
00:02:24,760 --> 00:02:29,400
hundreds, or thousands of similar
samples based on the original

31
00:02:29,400 --> 00:02:36,760
sample, then recalculate our variable
of interest. This lets us examine the

32
00:02:36,760 --> 00:02:43,180
distribution obtained from this
resampling and, for example, calculate

33
00:02:43,180 --> 00:02:47,020
the confidence interval around
our statistic of interest.

34
00:02:48,700 --> 00:02:52,900
So, what I wanted to explain, which I
put together for this course, you can

35
00:02:52,900 --> 00:02:58,020
find here, for example, on another
website, with hypothesis tests,

36
00:02:58,060 --> 00:03:01,900
the choice between parametric and nonparametric
methods, whether we have one or more

37
00:03:01,900 --> 00:03:05,160
samples to work with, and the
different tests we have seen.

38
00:03:05,160 --> 00:03:07,820
We have not covered all of
them, but we have covered many.

39
00:03:08,200 --> 00:03:11,800
Of course, others are
specific to particular types

40
00:03:11,800 --> 00:03:15,380
of situations, with many
options available for each test.

41
00:03:15,560 --> 00:03:19,760
But with what we have covered, I
think we cover about 80% of what you

42
00:03:19,760 --> 00:03:27,020
are likely to encounter in the literature
or need in your future studies.
